WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.065 --> 00:00:01.568
-Our next guest is an Emmy-

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:03.803
and Golden Globe-nominated
writer, director, and actress.

00:00:03.803 --> 00:00:06.672
You know her from her work on
"Killing Eve" and "The Crown."

00:00:06.672 --> 00:00:10.042
Her feature-film directorial
debut, "Promising Young Woman,"

00:00:10.042 --> 00:00:11.311
which she also wrote
and produced,

00:00:11.311 --> 00:00:13.546
has earned her three
Academy Award nominations

00:00:13.546 --> 00:00:17.116
for Best Picture, Best Director,
and Best Original Screenplay.

00:00:17.116 --> 00:00:18.117
Let's take a look.

00:00:18.117 --> 00:00:20.153
-May I ask what prompted
your desire

00:00:20.153 --> 00:00:21.921
to get back to your studies?

00:00:21.921 --> 00:00:26.592
-I guess...I couldn't stop
thinking about my time here.

00:00:26.592 --> 00:00:29.795
-Yeah,
it's an extraordinary place.

00:00:29.795 --> 00:00:31.664
It's an unusual request.

00:00:31.664 --> 00:00:35.167
-Yes, but I left under
unusual circumstances.

00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:36.602
-Oh.

00:00:36.602 --> 00:00:39.438
-I left because
of what happened to Nina.

00:00:39.438 --> 00:00:41.974
-Mm.
-Nina Fisher?

00:00:41.974 --> 00:00:44.210
You don't remember her?

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:47.814
Maybe you remember
Alexander Monroe?

00:00:47.814 --> 00:00:49.549
-Oh, yes!
Alexander Monroe.

00:00:49.549 --> 00:00:53.052
He actually just came back
and gave a talk here.

00:00:53.052 --> 00:00:55.187
Oh, he's a --
he's a really nice guy.

00:00:55.187 --> 00:00:56.255
Really smart.

00:00:56.255 --> 00:00:57.990
Are you a friend of his?
-No.

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:00.392
-Please welcome to the show
Emerald Fennell.

00:01:00.392 --> 00:01:02.295
How are you, Emerald?

00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:05.297
-I'm good, thank you.
I'm great.

00:01:05.297 --> 00:01:09.335
-Congratulations, obviously,
on all the nominations.

00:01:09.335 --> 00:01:12.538
I'm always curious to find out
where were you

00:01:12.538 --> 00:01:16.142
and how did you get the news?

00:01:16.142 --> 00:01:18.944
-Well, I was at home in England,

00:01:18.944 --> 00:01:23.416
and I made
a really terrible error,

00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:25.785
which was that
daylight savings time,

00:01:25.785 --> 00:01:29.088
which, at the best of times,
is the worst thing ever,

00:01:29.088 --> 00:01:31.023
it happened in America
but not in England.

00:01:31.023 --> 00:01:37.130
And so I was on a Zoom call when
I got a text from Josh O'Connor,

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:38.898
who plays Prince Charles
in "The Crown,"

00:01:38.898 --> 00:01:41.767
saying,
"Congratulations! Oscars!"

00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:44.070
I said, "What?"

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.105
And he's like,
"Why am I telling you this?

00:01:46.105 --> 00:01:48.341
You need to be watching this."

00:01:48.341 --> 00:01:53.513
And so then, my husband came in,
and he had the live feed,

00:01:53.513 --> 00:01:56.148
and, you know, I just --
I think I blacked out.

00:01:56.148 --> 00:01:57.916
I think there was
a lot of screaming.

00:01:57.916 --> 00:02:00.286
Like, a lot of screaming.

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:04.924
And it's like, I don't know.
Nobody expects this thing.

00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:06.792
Especially when you make
an independent film.

00:02:06.792 --> 00:02:09.895
You know, so, like, it's just
the most incredible thing ever.

00:02:09.895 --> 00:02:11.097
-It's an independent film.

00:02:11.097 --> 00:02:13.132
It's also an incredibly
unique film.

00:02:13.132 --> 00:02:18.504
When did this idea first occur
to you as a screenplay?

00:02:18.504 --> 00:02:20.607
-Well, I think, you know,
I've just been thinking a lot

00:02:20.607 --> 00:02:22.842
about growing up
and the kinds of things

00:02:22.842 --> 00:02:25.912
that were jokes
in movies and in TV shows.

00:02:25.912 --> 00:02:28.281
You know,
the concept of girls waking up

00:02:28.281 --> 00:02:31.116
with bodies next to them,
not knowing who they were.

00:02:31.116 --> 00:02:36.088
And, you know, guys spying
on women in their locker rooms

00:02:36.088 --> 00:02:37.390
and getting people drunk

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:40.193
or waiting for the drunk girl
at the end of the party.

00:02:40.193 --> 00:02:41.861
You know,
it was just, like, banter.

00:02:41.861 --> 00:02:44.363
It was just stuff
that was just completely normal.

00:02:44.363 --> 00:02:46.098
And that was, you know,

00:02:46.098 --> 00:02:47.733
regrettably,
part of all of our lives.

00:02:47.733 --> 00:02:50.603
And so, I think I've just been
thinking about that a lot,

00:02:50.603 --> 00:02:55.841
the kind of very disturbingly
commonplace stuff about consent.

00:02:55.841 --> 00:02:59.779
And, yeah, you know, the thing
that came into my head

00:02:59.779 --> 00:03:01.914
was a very, very drunk girl
lying on a bed

00:03:01.914 --> 00:03:05.084
being undressed by a guy,
you know, drunkenly saying,

00:03:05.084 --> 00:03:06.686
"What are you doing,
what are you doing?"

00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:10.122
And then suddenly sitting up and
saying, "What are you doing?"

00:03:10.122 --> 00:03:12.024
And that "What are you doing?"

00:03:12.024 --> 00:03:14.426
felt like the beginning
of the movie.

00:03:14.426 --> 00:03:17.062
-There -- This is a movie where
it's very important

00:03:17.062 --> 00:03:21.900
not to give anything away,
but I had read before I saw it

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:25.805
that this is one of the last
movies where there were

00:03:25.805 --> 00:03:29.575
actual screenings where people
saw it in a theater together

00:03:29.575 --> 00:03:33.179
and that an actual fight broke
out because there is a moment

00:03:33.179 --> 00:03:35.582
that people could see
very different ways

00:03:35.582 --> 00:03:37.650
or have very different
reactions to.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:38.984
Were you in the theater
when that happened,

00:03:38.984 --> 00:03:41.086
or did you hear about it?

00:03:41.086 --> 00:03:42.488
-No, I was in the theater.

00:03:42.488 --> 00:03:43.856
And it was my first
test screening.

00:03:43.856 --> 00:03:45.724
It was my only test screening
I've ever attended.

00:03:45.724 --> 00:03:48.894
And I sort of said to the
producers and the distributors

00:03:48.894 --> 00:03:51.997
who were all there,
"I'll go and sit at the back.

00:03:51.997 --> 00:03:54.567
I just want to really
hear everyone," you know?

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:56.969
"I really want to -- want to
really experience everything."

00:03:56.969 --> 00:03:59.238
And then, of course, I sat down,
and the guy next to me went,

00:03:59.238 --> 00:04:00.840
"What is this?"
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:03.142
And the woman right next to him
went, "I don't know."

00:04:03.142 --> 00:04:06.812
It's like, "Oh, no. Oh, no.
Why I am I sitting here?"

00:04:06.812 --> 00:04:09.682
And so I was very far away,
but what happened,

00:04:09.682 --> 00:04:11.717
there's a scene in the film,

00:04:11.717 --> 00:04:14.420
which is, you know,
very disturbing.

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:18.257
And somebody in the audience
liked it,

00:04:18.257 --> 00:04:20.860
and the other person did not.

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:22.895
And there was a lot of shouting.

00:04:22.895 --> 00:04:25.364
And my only thought was,
"Oh, great. Okay.

00:04:25.364 --> 00:04:27.700
Well, this movie
is never gonna be released."

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:30.936
-And yet --
And yet here you are.

00:04:30.936 --> 00:04:33.772
I also want to mention briefly

00:04:33.772 --> 00:04:37.910
or mostly just a compliment
your work on "The Crown."

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:40.012
Here you are
as Camilla Parker Bowles.

00:04:40.012 --> 00:04:43.416
Some of the best
"smoking in bathtub" acting

00:04:43.416 --> 00:04:46.185
we've seen
certainly in this century.

00:04:46.185 --> 00:04:48.421
Did you enjoy doing it?

00:04:48.421 --> 00:04:52.859
-Thank you so much.
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:52.859 --> 00:04:54.526
-Yes, that was one of the --

00:04:54.526 --> 00:04:57.329
that was a very, very difficult
performance for me.

00:04:57.329 --> 00:05:00.232
A real stretch.

00:05:00.232 --> 00:05:02.368
Look, actually, joking aside,

00:05:02.368 --> 00:05:06.139
it was very hard,
because the bath was very hot.

00:05:06.139 --> 00:05:10.376
And I had to smoke
30,000 cigarettes,

00:05:10.376 --> 00:05:14.080
and actually, by the end,
I remember

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:18.350
I sort of slid out of the bath
like a sort of beached whale.

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:21.687
And everyone in the room,
you know,

00:05:21.687 --> 00:05:24.590
usually people are discreetly
looking away politely,

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:26.592
but in this case,
they were sort of recoiling.

00:05:26.592 --> 00:05:28.261
[ Laughter ]

00:05:28.261 --> 00:05:30.295
It wasn't great
for the self-confidence.

00:05:30.295 --> 00:05:32.398
But, you know,
they were quite right to do so.

00:05:32.398 --> 00:05:34.366
-Well, hopefully,
there's plenty now

00:05:34.366 --> 00:05:36.735
for you to have nothing
but confidence about.

00:05:36.735 --> 00:05:38.804
Best of luck
at the Academy Awards.

00:05:38.804 --> 00:05:40.939
And thank you so much
for making time for us today.

00:05:40.939 --> 00:05:43.075
-Of course.
Thank you so much.

00:05:43.075 --> 00:05:45.677
-"Promising Young Woman"
is in theaters and on demand

00:05:45.677 --> 00:05:47.747
and available to own
on digital and DVD.

